---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { SITE, POLICY_LAST_UPDATED } from '../consts';

/**
 * Text is taken from docs/privacy-policy.md in the app repo, with "Expense
 * Tracker" replaced by the product name and the placeholder date filled in.
 * When that file changes, change this page in the same commit and bump
 * POLICY_LAST_UPDATED.
 *
 * The ids below are also the anchors on the <h2>s further down. Keep them
 * matched: a renamed heading id silently breaks a contents link.
 */
const toc = [
  { id: 'controller', label: 'Who is responsible' },
  { id: 'device', label: 'Data on your device' },
  { id: 'icloud', label: 'iCloud Sync' },
  { id: 'sent', label: 'What the app sends' },
  { id: 'basis', label: 'Legal basis (EU/UK)' },
  { id: 'transfers', label: 'International transfers' },
  { id: 'retention', label: 'How long data is kept' },
  { id: 'rights', label: 'Your rights' },
  { id: 'children', label: 'Children' },
  { id: 'changes', label: 'Changes to this policy' },
  { id: 'contact', label: 'Contact' },
] as const;

const mailto = `mailto:${SITE.email}`;
---

<Layout
  title={`Privacy Policy · ${SITE.name}`}
  description={`How ${SITE.name} handles your data: your transactions stay on your phone, or in your own iCloud if you turn on sync, and the two things that reach anyone else can be switched off.`}
  current="privacy"
>
  <div class="section">
    <div class="shell shell--narrow">
      <h1 class="t-h1-doc">Privacy Policy</h1>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[15px] text-text3">
        Last updated: {POLICY_LAST_UPDATED} &middot; Applies to {SITE.name} for
        iOS and Android
      </p>

      <div class="card mt-10 px-7 py-8 sm:px-9">
        <h2 class="text-[13px] font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.1em] text-accent">
          The short version
        </h2>
        <p class="mt-4 text-[clamp(1.0625rem,1rem+0.24vw,1.1563rem)] leading-[1.65] text-text">
          Your transactions stay on your phone. There is no account, no
          sign-in, and no server of ours holding your data. If you turn on
          iCloud Sync, they are also kept in your own iCloud account, encrypted
          before they leave the device, so your other iPhones can share them.
          We never see them either way.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-3.5 text-[clamp(1rem,0.977rem+0.1vw,1.0313rem)] leading-[1.7] text-text2">
          The only information that leaves your device is anonymous usage counts
          and, if the app crashes, a technical report describing what went
          wrong. Both can be switched off in Settings, and both are on by
          default so a bug can be found and fixed before you have to report it
          yourself.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="mt-14 flex flex-col gap-10 lg:flex-row lg:gap-15">
        <!-- Contents. A disclosure on small screens so it does not push the
             policy itself below the fold; a sticky rail on desktop. -->
        <nav class="lg:hidden" aria-label="Contents">
          <details class="card rounded-[var(--radius-inner)] px-5 py-4">
            <summary class="cursor-pointer text-[15px] font-semibold text-text">
              Contents
            </summary>
            <ul class="mt-2">
              {
                toc.map((item) => (
                  <li>
                    <a href={`#${item.id}`} class="toc-link">{item.label}</a>
                  </li>
                ))
              }
            </ul>
          </details>
        </nav>

        <nav
          class="hidden shrink-0 lg:sticky lg:top-24 lg:block lg:h-fit lg:w-[200px]"
          aria-label="Contents"
        >
          <p class="label-caps mb-3">Contents</p>
          <ul>
            {
              toc.map((item) => (
                <li>
                  <a href={`#${item.id}`} class="toc-link">{item.label}</a>
                </li>
              ))
            }
          </ul>
        </nav>

        <div class="prose-policy min-w-0 lg:max-w-[680px]">
          <h2 id="controller">Who is responsible for your data</h2>
          <p>
            {SITE.person}, based in Vietnam, is the data controller for {SITE.name}.
            You can reach me at <a href={mailto}>{SITE.email}</a>.
          </p>

          <h2 id="device">Your transactions stay on your device</h2>
          <p>
            <strong>
              {SITE.name} never sends your transactions, categories, accounts,
              or budgets to us. There is no server of ours and no account
              holding them.
            </strong>
          </p>
          <p>
            Everything you record is saved inside the app’s own storage on your
            device, in an encrypted local database. It is included in your
            device’s normal iCloud or Android backup, the same as any other
            app’s files. The only way {SITE.name} itself moves your records off
            the device is iCloud Sync, and only if you turn it on.
          </p>
          <p>
            A backup you create with Backup &amp; Restore is a file you choose
            where to send: saved to your device, shared through the system share
            sheet, or kept wherever you put it. It goes where you send it, not
            to us.
          </p>

          <h2 id="icloud">iCloud Sync, off unless you turn it on</h2>
          <p>
            <strong>
              Available on iPhone with iOS 17 or later. Off until you switch it
              on in Settings &rsaquo; iCloud Sync.
            </strong>
          </p>
          <p>
            When it is on, your transactions, categories, accounts, budgets,
            currencies, and exchange rates are stored as records in the private
            database of your own iCloud account, using Apple’s CloudKit service.
            That is how every iPhone signed in to the same Apple Account keeps
            the same records. Your app settings, including the app lock and its
            passcode, do not sync.
          </p>
          <p>
            The contents of each record are encrypted on your device before they
            are sent, with keys kept in your iCloud Keychain. Apple stores the
            records and can see that they exist, how many there are, their sizes,
            and when they changed, but not what they contain. We have no access
            to your iCloud account or to anything stored in it.
          </p>
          <p>
            Who can read synced records: your own devices, and anyone who can
            sign in to your Apple Account and also unlock your iCloud Keychain
            (for example, with the passcode of one of your trusted devices).
            Protect your Apple Account the way you would protect the phone
            itself.
          </p>
          <p>
            Apple’s handling of iCloud data is covered by Apple’s own privacy
            policy and iCloud terms, not by this policy.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>To stop it:</strong> turning off the switch in Settings
            &rsaquo; iCloud Sync pauses sync; what is already in iCloud stays
            there so you can resume later. Delete iCloud Data, on the same
            screen, removes your records from iCloud. Neither deletes anything
            from your device.
          </p>

          <h2 id="sent">What {SITE.name} does send</h2>
          <p>
            Two services receive information from the app. Neither receives your
            transactions, your amounts, your dates, your notes, your account or
            category names, or anything that identifies you.
          </p>

          <h3>1. Anonymous usage statistics, can be switched off</h3>
          <p><strong>Service:</strong> TelemetryDeck (Germany, servers in the EU)</p>
          <p>
            To understand which parts of the app are actually used and worth
            improving, {SITE.name} records events like: a transaction was saved
            (and whether it was an expense, income, or transfer, never the
            amount or note), a date range was changed, a filter was applied, an
            entity such as a category or account was created. Alongside each
            event: the app’s version, your device’s general model (such as
            “iPhone 15 Pro”), its operating system version, and your device’s
            language.
          </p>
          <p>
            What it never records: amounts, dates, ids, names, tags, notes, the
            search query, currency codes, or anything that identifies your
            specific device (such as the name you gave it).
          </p>
          <p>
            TelemetryDeck identifies a device using a random id generated on
            this device, which is hashed again by TelemetryDeck with a
            daily-rotating salt, so the same device cannot be recognized across
            days, by us or by them.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>To switch it off:</strong> Settings &rsaquo; Privacy &rsaquo;
            Share Usage Data. Data stops immediately.
          </p>

          <h3>2. Crash reports, can be switched off</h3>
          <p><strong>Service:</strong> Sentry (Germany, EU data region)</p>
          <p>
            When the app crashes or hits an unexpected error, a report is sent
            so the bug can be found and fixed. It contains the technical state
            of the app at the moment it failed: the code location, your device
            model, OS version, and a short trail of recent app activity such as
            “the database could not be opened.”
          </p>
          <p>
            It does not contain your transactions, amounts, notes, or anything
            you typed.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>To switch it off:</strong> Settings &rsaquo; Privacy &rsaquo;
            Share Crash Reports. Collection stops immediately, and any report
            already queued on the device is deleted rather than sent.
          </p>

          <h2 id="basis">Legal basis for processing (EU/UK users)</h2>
          <div class="table-scroll">
            <table>
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="col">What</th>
                  <th scope="col">Why</th>
                  <th scope="col">Legal basis</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr>
                  <td>Transactions, categories, accounts, budgets</td>
                  <td>To provide the app’s core function</td>
                  <td>
                    Processed only on your device, not shared with us at all
                  </td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>Records synced with iCloud Sync</td>
                  <td>To keep your devices in step, only if you turn it on</td>
                  <td>
                    Your choice; stored by Apple in your own iCloud account under
                    your agreement with Apple, not accessible to us
                  </td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>Anonymous usage statistics</td>
                  <td>
                    To understand which features are worth keeping and improving
                  </td>
                  <td>
                    Anonymized, so outside the scope of data-protection law; a
                    switch is offered regardless
                  </td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>Crash reports</td>
                  <td>To find and fix defects and keep the app stable</td>
                  <td>
                    Legitimate interest in a working, reliable app, with an
                    opt-out
                  </td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>

          <h2 id="transfers">International transfers</h2>
          <p>
            Both TelemetryDeck and Sentry store data in the EU (TelemetryDeck in
            Germany; Sentry’s EU data region, hosted in Frankfurt). Sentry is a
            US-incorporated company, so although the data itself is stored in
            the EU, the processing relationship is covered by Sentry’s data
            processing agreement and standard contractual clauses rather than
            the data simply never crossing a border.
          </p>
          <p>
            If you turn on iCloud Sync, Apple decides where your iCloud data is
            stored, under its own privacy policy. It is encrypted before it
            leaves your device.
          </p>

          <h2 id="retention">How long data is kept</h2>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <strong>Your transactions and other records:</strong> on your
              device, until you delete them. If you turn on iCloud Sync, also in
              your own iCloud account until you delete them or use Delete iCloud
              Data. There is no copy anywhere else.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Usage statistics:</strong> retained by TelemetryDeck under
              its own retention policy. Because the identifier rotates daily,
              older data cannot be connected to newer data.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Crash reports:</strong> retained by Sentry for 30 days.
            </li>
          </ul>

          <h2 id="rights">Your rights</h2>
          <p>
            If you are in the EU, UK, or another region with similar law, you
            have the right to access, correct, delete, restrict, or object to the
            processing of your personal data, and to receive it in a portable
            form.
          </p>
          <p>
            In practice, most of these rights have nothing to act on: {SITE.name}
            {' '}holds no account and no personal profile, your data is on your own
            device (and, with iCloud Sync on, in your own iCloud account) and
            outside our reach, and the usage statistics are anonymized
            so they cannot be traced back to you or retrieved individually.
          </p>
          <p>
            For crash reports, you can stop future collection at any time using
            the Settings switch. To request deletion of past reports, contact us
            at the address above and we will pass the request to Sentry.
          </p>
          <p>
            You also have the right to lodge a complaint with your local data
            protection authority.
          </p>

          <h2 id="children">Children</h2>
          <p>
            {SITE.name} is not directed at children under 13 and does not
            knowingly collect information from them.
          </p>

          <h2 id="changes">Changes to this policy</h2>
          <p>
            If this policy changes, the updated version will be published at
            this address with a new “last updated” date. Significant changes to
            what {SITE.name} collects will be described in the app’s release
            notes.
          </p>

          <h2 id="contact">Contact</h2>
          <p>
            {SITE.author}<br />
            <a href={mailto}>{SITE.email}</a>
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
